description:
Use this to save code as a Sidekick action the user can run again by name, from this or any other assistant, and from the Sidekick Actions panel in InDesign when the user's Sidekick plugin has it. Save only when the user asks, or agrees after you offered; never on your own initiative. When saving a fix to an action that failed, say what changed.

The source is one JavaScript file: a header, then an async function main(params, sidekick) and any helpers it needs. Saving under an existing name replaces that action.

/* @sidekick
{
  "name": "alt-text-audit",
  "description": "Lists every placed image without alt text",
  "params": {
    "scope": { "type": "string", "enum": ["document", "selection"], "default": "document",
               "description": "Which images to check" }
  }
}
*/
function hasAltText(frame) {
  const options = frame.objectExportOptions;
  const source = String(options.altTextSourceType);
  if (source === "SOURCE_CUSTOM") return options.customAltText.trim() !== "";
  return source !== "SOURCE_XML_STRUCTURE"; // InDesign's default; for an untagged document it means none
}

async function main({ scope }, sidekick) {
  const { app } = require("indesign");
  const doc = sidekick.doc();
  const graphics = scope === "selection" ? app.selection : doc.allGraphics;
  const items = [];
  for (const graphic of graphics) {
    const frame = graphic.parent;
    if (hasAltText(frame)) continue;
    const image = graphic.itemLink ? graphic.itemLink.name : "embedded image";
    items.push(sidekick.item(frame, image, "No alt text"));
  }
  return {
    summary: items.length === 0 ? "All images have alt text" : items.length + " images without alt text",
    items,
  };
}

Header rules: name is kebab-case (a lower-case letter, then 1-63 lower-case letters, digits or hyphens); description is one line of at most 200 characters, best about 100: what the action does, the most important first, without repeating what a param's description says (the Sidekick Actions panel shows it above the param fields); params is optional, each with type "string", "number" or "boolean", and optionally description, default, enum (strings only) or required (not together with default). Param names are JavaScript identifiers. An action that can both report and change takes a boolean param that defaults to false and makes it change the document, and names it in the header as "apply": "<param name>"; with it off, the action changes nothing and reports what it would change. Such an action is not finished until it has run in both modes: run it once with the param off, then ask the user for one trial run with it on that is undone right away (Edit › Undo), or run that trial on a copy of the document; undo it only when run_action's answer shows an Undo line, and use a copy otherwise. Only then say the action is ready. The header cannot contain */. Unknown header fields are errors.

sidekick.doc() returns the active document or throws "No document is open"; sidekick.resolve(specifier, label) throws "<label> not found" for an itemByName/itemByID that matched nothing; sidekick.item(pageItem, label, detail) makes a row the user can click to go to that item (pass the frame for a table cell; an id is accepted instead of the item; a Text object (a Find/Change hit, a paragraph or a character range) makes a row that selects that text); sidekick.fail(message) stops with a message about the user's situation ("Select one or more images first") rather than a fault in the action; sidekick.log(text) adds a line to the log returned with the report.

main returns a report: { summary, items, changeCount, data }. summary is one line, required ("3 frames with overset text"). items is a list of rows: sidekick.item(...) for a place, { label, count } for a tally, { label, detail } for a fact. changeCount is how many changes the run made, or would make when the apply param is off. data holds anything else, for you; the panel shows summary, items and changeCount, never data. A bare string is read as the summary. Change the document before any await: where a run is one undo step (the panel, and the chat with a plugin that has it), that holds only up to the first await. Keep actions small and take what changes from run to run as params.
